Article 60
(1) In the event it is necessary, the Regency Government and the Community may undertake prevention efforts so that Children do not become victims of pornography.
(2) Prevention efforts by the Regency Government as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through:
a. formulation and implementation of policies related to regional action plans on the prevention and handling of pornography;
b. optimization of the role of the Task Force for Prevention and Handling of Pornography;
c. capacity building of the Regional Task Force for Prevention and Handling of Pornography;
d. formulation and dissemination of communication, information, and education materials related to pornography prevention in schools and the Community;
e. guidance to the Community not to engage in activities that lead to pornography; and
f. guidance to Regional Apparatus, non-governmental organizations, and the Community to enhance efforts for the prevention and handling of pornography.
(3) Prevention efforts by the Community so that Children do not become Victims of Pornography shall be carried out through:
a. dissemination of communication, information, and education materials related to pornography prevention in the surrounding environment;
b. strengthening of the surrounding environment not to engage in activities that lead to pornography; and
c. enhancement of prevention and handling efforts of pornography in the surrounding environment.
(4) Provisions regarding the procedures for prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (2) and paragraph (3) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 73
Prevention as referred to in Article 70 is carried out by means of:
a. involving the Community in carrying out Special Protection of Children;
b. increasing understanding related to kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking of Children;
c. establishing bilateral and multilateral cooperation, both national and international;
d. strengthening family resilience to prevent Children from kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking; and
e. increasing the responsibility of the Community, business sector, and mass media to protect Children from kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking.

Article 36
(1) Special Protection for Children in Conflict with the Law as referred to in Article 32 paragraph (1) letter b shall be carried out through:
a. humane treatment with attention to needs according to their age;
b. separation from adults;
c. provision of legal assistance and other assistance effectively;
d. implementation of recreational activities;
e. freedom from torture, punishment, or other treatment that is cruel, inhumane, and degrading to dignity and degree;
f. avoidance of imposition of the death penalty and/or life imprisonment;
g. avoidance of arrest, detention or imprisonment, except as a last resort and for the shortest period of time;
h. provision of trial before a Children's Court that is objective, impartial, and in a session closed to the public;
i. avoidance of publication of their identity;
j. provision of accompaniment by parents/guardians and persons trusted by the Child;
k. provision of social advocacy;
l. provision of private life;
m. provision of accessibility, especially for Children with Disabilities;
n. provision of education;
o. provision of health services; and
p. provision of other rights in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(2) Special Protection for Children in Conflict with the Law as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through efforts of:
a. prevention;
b. administrative settlement of cases;
c. rehabilitation; and
d. social reintegration.

Article 38
(1) Prevention efforts so that children do not come into contact with the law as referred to in Article 36 paragraph (2) letter a are carried out so that:
a. Children do not become victims of criminal acts;
b. Children do not come into conflict with the Law; and
c. Children no longer commit criminal acts.
(2) Prevention so that children do not come into contact with the Law as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out through:
a. print media, electronic media, and online media;
b. face-to-face in the form of counseling, discussions, lectures, campaigns; and
c. outdoor media.
(3) Prevention efforts so that Children do not come into conflict with the law as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b are carried out through:
a. dissemination;
b. providing places or spaces for play, healthy recreation, and channeling Children's creativity;
c. providing guidance on religious values and social values;
d. conducting supervision of environments that will have an impact on the occurrence of Children who come into contact with the Law;
e. increasing family resilience and welfare;
f. providing coaching and skills training;
g. providing places or spaces for play, healthy recreation, and channeling Children's creativity;
h. providing religious guidance, social values, and good character;
i. developing an environment that cares for Children in Contact with the Law; and/or
j. involving families in service, coaching, and guidance programs.

Article 80
(1) Dissemination and socialization of the provisions of laws and regulations that protect Children who are victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 74 shall be carried out by the Regency Government.
(2) Monitoring and reporting as referred to in Article 78 shall be carried out by the Regency Government.
(3) Implementation of Special Protection as referred to in Article 79 shall be carried out by:
a. the Regency Government through the Regional Apparatus (Perangkat Daerah) that administers governmental affairs in the field of education;
b. educators;
c. education personnel;
d. family; and/or
e. the Community, in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 59
Physical and mental health recovery as referred to in Article 55 paragraph (1) shall be provided in the form of:
a. psycho-social therapy;
b. counseling;
c. beneficial activities;
d. referral to hospitals, safe houses, service centers, or other alternative places in accordance with needs; and/or
e. resocialization.

